    {
     "author": {
      "accountId": "557058:47933046-2ef5-496c-8f27-de69c527efc0",
      "accountType": "atlassian",
      "active": true,
      "avatarUrls": {
       "16x16": "https://avatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net/initials/AE-5.png",
       "24x24": "https://avatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net/initials/AE-5.png",
       "32x32": "https://avatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net/initials/AE-5.png",
       "48x48": "https://avatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net/initials/AE-5.png"
      },
      "displayName": "Aaron Elkiss",
      "self": "https://fedora-repository.atlassian.net/rest/api/3/user?accountId=557058%3A47933046-2ef5-496c-8f27-de69c527efc0",
      "timeZone": "America/Halifax"
     },
     "body": {
      "content": [
       {
        "content": [
         {
          "text": "There are some issues with this test as designed - the default HTTP request sampler in JMeter keeps the entire request in memory at once; additionally individual arrays have limited size in Java so it can't even create an individual request with a 10GB random file attached. I was able to create up to about 500MB, and that still needed a heap size for JMeter of 8GB. If we want to run this plan as specified, it would need a custom sampler in JMeter that generates reasonable-sized chunks of large files rather than the entire thing at once.",
          "type": "text"
         }
        ],
        "type": "paragraph"
       },
       {
        "content": [
         {
          "text": "Additionally, creating 1000s of multi-gigabyte files is going to require some serious storage capacity and take quite a lot of time; it might make sense to run the performance requests more frequently for this test than every 1000th request.",
          "type": "text"
         }
        ],
        "type": "paragraph"
       }
      ],
      "type": "doc",
      "version": 1
     },
